## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests advanced dynamic programming: partitioning with a non-trivial segment cost that depends on min and max, requiring careful state design and in-loop maintenance of subproblem results. It also validates producing both memoized and iterative solutions and analyzing time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- DP with partition states
- In-loop maintenance of running min and max
- Top-down memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Time complexity: O(D*N^2); Space: O(D*N)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized
function minCost(A, D){
  const N = A.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(null));
  function solve(d, i){
    if (d === 1){
      let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity;
      for (let k = 0; k <= i; k++){ mn = Math.min(mn, A[k]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[k]); }
      return mn * mx;
    }
    if (memo[d][i] != null) return memo[d][i];
    let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity; let best = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      best = Math.min(best, solve(d-1, j-1) + mn*mx);
    }
    memo[d][i] = best; return best;
  }
  return solve(D, N-1);
}

// Bottom-up
function minCostBottom(A, D){
  const N = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(INF));
  // base: d = 1
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++){
    let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      if (j === 0) dp[1][i] = Math.min(dp[1][i], mn*mx);
    }
  }
  for (let d = 2; d <= D; d++){
    for (let i = d-1; i < N; i++){
      let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
      for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
        mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        dp[d][i] = Math.min(dp[d][i], dp[d-1][j-1] + mn*mx);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[D][N-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Autocomplete for large vocabularies is common; a Trie yields fast prefix queries and ordered counts.

## Key Concepts
- Trie vs BST for prefix-based lookup
- DFS with pruning and max-heap to collect top-k results
- Memory optimizations: sparse maps, compact nodes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.word = null;
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word, delta) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) {
        cur.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }
    cur.word = word;
    cur.freq += delta;
  }

  search(word) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) return false;
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }
    return cur.word === word;
  }

  prefixTop(prefix, k) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) return [];
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (node) => {
      if (node.word) {
        results.push({ word: node.word, freq: node.freq });
        if (results.length > k) {
          results.sort((a, b) => b.freq - a.freq);
          results.pop();
        }
      }
      for (const child of node.next.values()) {
        dfs(child);
      }
    };

    dfs(cur);
    return results.sort((a, b) => b.freq - a.freq).slice(0, k).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

## Time/Space Trade-offs vs BST/AVL
- **Trie**: O(m) insert/search where m = word length, independent of vocabulary size. Prefix queries O(m + k) where k = results. Space O(α×m) where α = alphabet size.
- **BST/AVL**: O(log n) operations where n = vocabulary size. Prefix queries require O(n) traversal. Space O(n).
- **Trie advantage**: Constant-time prefix lookups, natural ordering by frequency, better cache locality for common prefixes.
- **BST advantage**: More memory efficient for sparse vocabularies, simpler implementation.

## Memory-heavy Vocabulary Justification
For large vocabularies, use:
- **Sparse maps** (Map/Object) instead of fixed arrays for children
- **Node compression** (radix/patricia tries) to merge common prefixes
- **Frequency-based pruning** to remove low-frequency words
- **External storage** for very large datasets, keeping hot prefixes in memory

Tries excel when prefix queries dominate and memory can be optimized through compression techniques.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DP with a multi‑dimensional state and non‑trivial transitions, mixing path constraints with exact move counts. It also probes memoization vs bottom‑up, boundary handling, and memory optimizations essential for large grids.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) representing position and moves
- Transitions from (i-1,j,t-1), (i,j-1,t-1), (i-1,j-1,t-1)
- -INF for invalid states; base cases
- Memory optimization via rolling arrays
- Trade-offs between readability (memoized DFS) and performance (tabulation)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridSum(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array.from({length:m}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  function dfs(i,j,t){
    if(i<0||j<0||t<0) return NEG;
    if(i===0&&j===0&&t===0) return grid[0][0];
    if(memo[i][j][t]!==NEG) return memo[i][j][t];
    const best = Math.max(dfs(i-1,j,t-1), dfs(i,j-1,t-1), dfs(i-1,j-1,t-1));
    memo[i][j][t] = (best===NEG)? NEG : grid[i][j] + best;
    return memo[i][j][t];
  }
  return dfs(n-1,m-1,k);
}
```

